.TH "cbor::sequence_reader" "3" "2026-10-31" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH NAME
cbor::sequence_reader \- gives the items of a CBOR sequence whose bytes arrive in pieces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<cbor/cbor.hpp>\f[R]
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] sequence_reader
{
\f[B]public\f[R]:
    sequence_reader();
    sequence_reader(sequence_reader &&);
    sequence_reader &\f[B]operator\f[R]=(sequence_reader &&);

    \f[B]template\f[R] <borrowed_range_of_chars Piece>
    void feed(Piece piece);
    \f[B]template\f[R] <owning_range_of_chars Piece>
    void feed(Piece const &piece);
    \f[B]template\f[R] <owning_range_of_chars Piece>
    void feed(Piece &&piece);
    \f[B]template\f[R] <owning_range_of_chars Piece>
    void feed(std::shared_ptr<Piece const> piece);

    std::expected<lazy, error> next();
    bool empty() const;
};
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads a CBOR sequence (RFC 8742) whose bytes arrive in pieces, for
example from a socket.
\f[CR]feed\f[R] gives the reader the next piece.
\f[CR]next\f[R] gives the next item as a \f[CR]lazy\f[R] when all of its
bytes are there, and \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] while it waits.
\f[CR]empty\f[R] is true when the reader holds no byte that no item
took.
.PP
\f[CR]feed(std::move(s))\f[R] takes a \f[CR]std::string\f[R] without a
copy.
\f[CR]feed(shared_ptr)\f[R] holds the piece without a copy.
\f[CR]feed(view)\f[R] and \f[CR]feed(s)\f[R] copy the bytes.
An item whole in a piece that the reader holds is a view into that
piece.
The reader copies an item that the end of a piece cuts, and only the
bytes of that item.
.PP
Each item keeps its bytes alive after later feeds and after the reader
is gone.
A char array, such as a literal, does not compile, because it holds a
NUL after its last byte.
A \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] to bytes that are not const does not
compile.
The reader cannot be copied.
.SH ERRORS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: the item is not complete yet.
Feed more bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]syntax_error\f[R], \f[CR]indefinite_length\f[R]: the bytes are not
a well\-formed item with definite lengths.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R]: the item is nested deeper than
\f[CR]limits::nesting_depth\f[R].
.PP
After any other error than \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R], every call of
\f[CR]next\f[R] gives that error, and \f[CR]feed\f[R] keeps no byte.
A null \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] and a call on a reader that was moved
from throw \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SH NOTES
Each byte is scanned once.
Memory O(n) of the bytes not yet read.
A feed and a \f[CR]next\f[R] allocate nothing once the buffer has grown,
except \f[CR]feed(std::move(s))\f[R], which allocates the block that
holds \f[CR]s\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
cbor::sequence_reader r;
r.feed(std::move(first));     \f[I]// 82 01: the start of [1, 2]\f[R]
r.next();                     \f[I]// too_little_data\f[R]
r.feed(std::move(second));    \f[I]// 02 03\f[R]
r.next()\->size();             \f[I]// 2\f[R]
r.next()\->get<std::int64_t>(); \f[I]// 3\f[R]
r.empty();                    \f[I]// true\f[R]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
mbedded\-cbor(3), cbor::lazy(3), cbor::at_path(3), cbor::item(3), cbor::structs(3), cbor::schema(3), cbor::ref(3), cbor::item_size(3), cbor::sequence(3), cbor::diagnostic_notation(3), cbor::error(3)
